  "legacyId": "7cfbdffd-d4f6-4692-876d-dd9ef1c90acb",
  "type": "problem",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "description": "<div><p>Start at index 0 of nums. From index i, a move may advance by any positive number of positions up to nums[i], without passing the final index. Find the smallest number of moves needed to reach the last index.</p><p>The last index is guaranteed to be reachable. If the array has one element, you are already at the destination and need zero moves.</p><p><strong>Example 1</strong></p><pre>Input: nums = [2,3,1,1,4]\nOutput: 2</pre><p>Explanation: Jump to index 1, then use its reach of three to land at index 4.</p><p><strong>Example 2</strong></p><pre>Input: nums = [2,3,0,1,4]\nOutput: 2</pre><p>Explanation: Index 1 can jump directly to the end despite the zero at index 2.</p><p><strong>Example 3</strong></p><pre>Input: nums = [0]\nOutput: 0</pre><p>Explanation: The starting index is also the final index.</p><p><strong>Constraints</strong></p><ul><li>1 ≤ nums.length ≤ 10,000.</li><li>0 ≤ nums[i] ≤ 10,000.</li><li>A path from index 0 to index nums.length - 1 always exists.</li></ul></div>",
  "templates": {
    "python": "class Solution:\n    def jump(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Return the requested result.\"\"\"\n        pass\n",
    "javascript": "var jump = function(nums) {\n    // your code here\n};\n"
  },
